    "content": "The University of Pennsylvania (Penn) is a private Ivy League research university in Philadelphia, founded in 1740 by Benjamin Franklin. Known for its elite business (Wharton), medical, and nursing schools, it is highly selective with a 5.4% acceptance rate and ranked #7 nationally. Tuition is roughly $66,104 annually. Key Academic & Admission Information: Admissions: Highly selective with a 5.4% acceptance rate for recent cycles. SAT/ACT: Admitted students typically score in the top ranges, with 2023-24 averages between 740-770 for Reading/Writing and 770-800 for Math. Tuition: For the 2023-2024 academic year, tuition was approximately $66,104. Academic Reputation: Ranked #7 among national universities. Key schools include the Wharton School (business), Perelman School of Medicine, and Nursing. Environment: Urban campus located in West Philadelphia, characterized by the historic Locust Walk. Key Facts about Penn: Research Focus: Over $800 million in research funding, with strengths in medicine, technology, and interdisciplinary studies. History: Founded in 1740 as one of the nation's first165 research centers and institutes. Demographics: Diverse student body with roughly 13% international students. Distinction: Known as one of the most practical Ivy League schools, focusing on entrepreneurship and applied research. Note: This information refers to the University of Pennsylvania (UPenn) in Philadelphia, not Pennsylvania State University.",
